+++
title: Koine Greek
description: Dialect of Greek in the ancient world
hook: A dialect that arose in the armies of Alexander the Great ended up
  spoken from Ptolemaic Egypt to Seleucid Mesopotamia, replacing existing
  Greek dialects with an everyday form that people anywhere could understand.
standfirst: The common form of Greek spoken and written during the Hellenistic
  period, the Roman Empire and the early Byzantine Empire. It spread with
  Alexander the Great's conquests in the fourth century BC and served as the
  lingua franca of much of the Mediterranean region and the Middle East.
key-facts:
  - name: In use
    value: c. 300 BC – AD 600 (developed into Medieval Greek, survives as the
      liturgical language of the Greek Orthodox and the Greek Catholic
      churches)
  - name: Spoken across
    value: Hellenistic Greece, Macedon, Ptolemaic Kingdom, Seleucid Empire and
      other Hellenistic Kingdoms, Roman Empire, Eastern Roman Empire
  - name: Spoken by
    value: Greeks
  - name: Written in
    value: Greek alphabet
  - name: Where it sits in the family
    value: Hellenic → Greek → Attic–Ionic → Attic → Koine Greek; earlier forms
      Proto-Greek and Ancient Greek
  - name: Language codes
    value: Glottolog koin1234; a proposal to use the ISO 639-3 code ecg was
      rejected in 2023
pull-quotes:
  - text: In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word
      was God.
    attribution: the beginning of the Gospel of John, in the translation given
      with the Koine sample
    section: sample-2--greek-new-testament
  - text: Concerning those matters about which the citizens of Thisbae made
      representations.
    attribution: a decree of the Roman Senate to the town of Thisbae in Boeotia in 170 BC
    section: sample-1--a-roman-decree
  - text: He maintains that ἐκκλησία is merely used for designating the notion of
      meeting and gathering of men, without any particular character.
      Therefore, etymologizing this word could be needless, or even
      misleading, when it could guide to false meanings, for example that
      ἐκκλησία is a name used for the people of God, Israel.
    attribution: Kyriakoula Papademetriou, explaining James Barr's criticism of
      etymological arguments
    section: septuagint-greek
timeline:
  - when: early 4th century BC
    what: Macedonian coinage of Amyntas III attests the first-declension genitive
      in -as (ΑΜΥΝΤΑ) that Koine took up in place of the Attic ΑΜΥΝΤΟΥ.
    from: Among the many contributions were the general use of the [[ first
      declension ]] grammar for male and female nouns with an -as ending,
      attested in the [[ genitive | genitive-case ]] of Macedonian coinage
      from the early 4th century BC of [[ Amyntas III | amyntas-iii-of-macedon
      ]] (ΑΜΥΝΤΑ in the genitive; the Attic form that fell into disuse would
      be ΑΜΥΝΤΟΥ).
  - when: 4th century BC
    what: Wikidata's start time for Koine Greek.
    from: "start-time: 4th century BC"
  - when: 323 BC
    what: Alexander the Great dies; the post-Classical period of Greek is defined
      as beginning here, as cultures under Greek sway begin in turn to
      influence the language.
    from: the post-Classical period of Greek is defined as beginning with the
      death of Alexander the Great in 323 BC, when cultures under Greek sway
      in turn began to influence the language.
  - when: 303 BC
    what: The Successor kingdoms stand as mapped, before the battle of Ipsus.
    from: The Successor kingdoms before the battle of Ipsus, 303 BC
  - when: 3rd century BC
    what: The Septuagint, the Greek translation of the Hebrew Bible, is made in
      Koine.
    from: Koine is also the language of the [[ Septuagint ]] (the 3rd century BC
      Greek translation of the [[ Hebrew Bible ]]), the Christian [[ New
      Testament ]], and of most early Christian theological writing by the [[
      Church Fathers ]].
  - when: 170 BC
    what: The Roman Senate issues a decree to the town of Thisbae in Boeotia – one
      of the two sample texts.
    from: The following excerpt, from a decree of the Roman Senate to the town of
      [[ Thisbae ]] in [[ Boeotia ]] in 170 BC, is rendered in a reconstructed
      pronunciation representing a hypothetical conservative variety of
      mainland Greek Koiné in the early Roman period.[[ ^36 ]]
  - when: second century BC
    what: The ancient distinction between long and short vowels is gradually lost;
      from now on all vowels are isochronic, of equal length.
    from: The ancient distinction between long and short vowels was gradually
      lost, and from the second century BC all vowels were isochronic (having
      equal length).[[ ^26 ]][[ ^13 ]]
  - when: second century BC
    what: The Ancient Greek pitch accent is replaced with a stress accent.
    from: From the second century BC, the [[ Ancient Greek pitch accent |
      ancient-greek-accent ]] was replaced with a [[ stress accent |
      stress-linguistics ]].[[ ^26 ]][[ ^13 ]]
  - when: 1st century BC
    what: Koine Greek becomes a language of literature, and some people begin to
      distinguish a literary post-classical form from the vernacular.
    from: "When Koine Greek became a language of literature by the 1st century BC,
      some people distinguished two forms: written as the literary
      post-classical form (which should not be confused with [[ Atticism ]])
      and the [[ vernacular ]].[[ ^13 ]]"
  - when: 2nd century
    what: Apollonius Dyscolus and Aelius Herodianus use //koine// to mean the
      Proto-Greek language, while others use it for any vernacular Greek
      unlike the literary language.
    from: In the 2nd century, scholars such as [[ Apollonius Dyscolus ]] and [[
      Aelius Herodianus ]] maintained the term //koine// to refer to the [[
      Proto-Greek language ]], while others used it to refer to any vernacular
      form of Greek speech which differed somewhat from the literary
      language.[[ ^13 ]]
  - when: between 175 and 225
    what: The 'most probable date' of Papyrus 46, one of the oldest extant New
      Testament manuscripts in Greek.
    from: Papyrus 46 is one of the oldest extant New Testament manuscripts in
      Greek, written on papyrus, with its 'most probable date' between 175 and
      225.
  - when: 330 AD
    what: Constantine the Great founds Constantinople; the passage into Medieval
      Greek is sometimes dated from here, but often only from the end of late
      antiquity.
    from: The passage into the next period, known as [[ Medieval Greek ]], is
      sometimes dated from the foundation of [[ Constantinople ]] by [[
      Constantine the Great ]] in 330 AD, but often only from the end of [[
      late antiquity ]].
  - when: After the 4th century
    what: Christianity becomes the state church of the Roman Empire, and more
      learned registers of Koiné come to be used by Christian writers.
    from: After the 4th century, when Christianity became the [[ state church of
      the Roman Empire ]], more learned registers of Koiné also came to be
      used.[[ ^24 ]]
  - when: 6th century
    what: Wikidata's end time for Koine Greek.
    from: "end-time: 6th century"
  - when: 10th century AD
    what: υ/οι unround to merge with ι – the last of the changes known as iotacism.
    from: η (classically pronounced) was raised and merged with ι. In the 10th
      century AD, υ/οι unrounded to merge with ι. These changes are known as
      [[ iotacism ]].[[ ^13 ]]
image-plan:
  - file: Greek_Script_from_Rosetta_Stone_(36140196440).jpg
    role: hero
    section: lead
    caption: Greek script from the Rosetta Stone decree, issued in the Ptolemaic
      Kingdom in the 2nd century BC.
  - file: Diadoch.png
    role: inline
    section: origins-and-history
    caption: The Successor kingdoms before the battle of Ipsus, 303 BC. The
      armies' newly formed common variety was spoken from the Ptolemaic
      Kingdom of Egypt to the Seleucid Empire of Mesopotamia.
  - file: Linguistic_map_of_the_Roman_Empire_c._400.svg
    role: full
    section: origins-and-history
    caption: Linguistic map of the Roman Empire c. 400. In its eastern provinces
      the empire communicated almost exclusively in Greek, even in areas where
      Greek was not the dominant spoken language.
  - file: P46.jpg
    role: inline
    section: types
    caption: Papyrus 46, one of the oldest extant New Testament manuscripts in
      Greek, written on papyrus; its 'most probable date' is between 175 and
      225.
glossary:
  - term: Koine
    gloss: From ἡ κοινὴ διάλεκτος, 'the common dialect'; the Greek word κοινή
      itself means 'common'.
    from: The English-language name //Koine// is derived from the Koine Greek term
      ἡ κοινὴ διάλεκτος (//hē koinḕ diálektos//), meaning "the common
      dialect".[[ ^5 ]] The Greek word [[ κοινή |
      https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/κοινή#Ancient_Greek ]] (//koinḗ//) itself
      means "common".
  - term: Septuagint
    gloss: The 3rd-century BC Greek translation of the Hebrew Bible (Old
      Testament) and of texts not included in the Hebrew Bible.
    from: The [[ Septuagint ]], a 3rd-century BC Greek translation of the [[
      Hebrew Bible ]] (Old Testament) and [[ texts not included in the Hebrew
      Bible | development-of-the-hebrew-bible-canon ]];
  - term: Attic Koine
    gloss: The literary Koine of the Hellenistic age, which resembles Attic so
      closely that it is often called by this name.
    from: The literary Koine of the Hellenistic age resembles Attic in such a
      degree that it is often mentioned as //Attic Koine//.[[ ^13 ]]
  - term: Historical present
    gloss: Present-tense verbs used in some narrative sections of the New
      Testament for events that are in the past with respect to the speaker.
    from: The "[[ historical present ]]" tense is a term used for present tense
      verbs that are used in some narrative sections of the New Testament to
      describe events that are in the past with respect to the speaker.
  - term: Iotacism
    gloss: The name for the changes by which η was raised and merged with ι, and
      by which υ/οι unrounded to merge with ι in the 10th century AD.
    from: η (classically pronounced) was raised and merged with ι. In the 10th
      century AD, υ/οι unrounded to merge with ι. These changes are known as
      [[ iotacism ]].[[ ^13 ]]
  - term: Psilosis
    gloss: Loss of the rough breathing, which had already gone from the Ionic
      Greek varieties of Anatolia and the Aeolic Greek of Lesbos.
    from: "[[ Psilosis ]]: loss of [[ rough breathing ]], . Rough breathing had
      already been lost in the [[ Ionic Greek ]] varieties of [[ Anatolia ]]
      and the [[ Aeolic Greek ]] of [[ Lesbos ]].[[ ^13 ]]"
  - term: Patristic Greek
    gloss: A term sometimes used for the Greek written by the Greek Church
      Fathers, the Early Christian theologians in late antiquity.
    from: The term //patristic Greek// is sometimes used for the Greek written by
      the [[ Greek Church Fathers | church-fathers#greek-fathers ]], the [[
      Early Christian | early-christianity ]] theologians in late antiquity.
  - term: Isochronic
    gloss: "Having equal length: from the second century BC all Greek vowels were
      isochronic, the old long/short distinction having gradually been lost."
    from: The ancient distinction between long and short vowels was gradually
      lost, and from the second century BC all vowels were isochronic (having
      equal length).[[ ^26 ]][[ ^13 ]]
further:
  - slug: alexander-the-great
    why: Koine arose as a common dialect within his armies, and the post-Classical
      period of Greek is dated from his death in 323 BC.
  - slug: macedonia-ancient-kingdom
    why: It was under Macedon's leadership that the new common variety spread, and
      Ancient Macedonian contributed considerable elements to it.
  - slug: rosetta-stone
    why: Its Greek section is a Ptolemaic decree of the 2nd century BC – Koine as
      it was actually cut into stone.
anchors:
  hook: Koine [[ Greek | greek-language ]] arose as a common dialect within the
    armies of [[ Alexander the Great ]].[[ ^13 ]] Under the leadership of [[
    Macedon | macedonia-ancient-kingdom ]], their newly formed common variety
    was spoken from the [[ Ptolemaic Kingdom ]] of Egypt to the [[ Seleucid
    Empire ]] of [[ Mesopotamia ]].[[ ^13 ]] It replaced existing [[ ancient
    Greek dialects ]] with an everyday form that people anywhere could
    understand.[[ ^15 ]]
  standfirst: was the [[ common supra-regional form | koiné-language ]] of [[
    Greek | greek-language ]] spoken and written during the [[ Hellenistic
    period ]], the [[ Roman Empire ]], and the early [[ Byzantine Empire ]].
    It evolved from the spread of Greek following the conquests of [[
    Alexander the Great ]] in the fourth century BC, and served as the [[
    lingua franca ]] of much of the Mediterranean region and [[ the Middle
    East ]] during the following centuries.
