== Ancient Macedonian Contribution

As a consequence of the [[ Macedonians | ancient-macedonians ]]' role in the formation of Koine Greek, [[ Ancient Macedonian | ancient-macedonian-language ]] contributed considerable elements to the language, including some military terminology (διμοιρίτης, ταξίαρχος, ὑπασπισταί, etc.). Among the many contributions were the general use of the [[ first declension ]] grammar for male and female nouns with an -as ending, attested in the [[ genitive | genitive-case ]] of Macedonian coinage from the early 4th century BC of [[ Amyntas III | amyntas-iii-of-macedon ]] (ΑΜΥΝΤΑ in the genitive; the Attic form that fell into disuse would be ΑΜΥΝΤΟΥ). There were changes in verb conjugation such as in the [[ imperative | imperative-mood ]] δέξα //dexa// attested in Macedonian sling stones found in Asiatic battlefields, that became adopted in place of the Attic forms. Koine Greek established a [[ spirantisation ]] of beta, gamma, and delta, which has been attributed to the Macedonian influence.[[ ^34 ]] The term "Macedonian" ended up meaning the Koine Greek in classical sources.[[ ^35 ]]

== Sample Koine texts

The two texts below show differences from Attic Greek in all aspects – grammar, morphology, vocabulary – and can be inferred to show differences in phonology.

The comments that go with them illustrate the phonological development within the period of Koine. The phonetic transcriptions are tentative and are intended to illustrate two different stages in the reconstructed development: an early conservative variety still relatively close to Classical Attic, and a somewhat later, more progressive variety approaching Modern Greek in some respects.

== Sample 1 – A Roman decree

The following excerpt, from a decree of the Roman Senate to the town of [[ Thisbae ]] in [[ Boeotia ]] in 170 BC, is rendered in a reconstructed pronunciation representing a hypothetical conservative variety of mainland Greek Koiné in the early Roman period.[[ ^36 ]] The transcription shows raising of η, partial (pre-consonantal/word-final) raising of ῃ and ει, retention of pitch accent, and retention of the word-initial [[ rough breathing ]].

<p lang="grc-x-koine">περὶ ὧν Θισ[β]εῖς λόγους ἐποιήσαντο· περὶ τῶν καθ᾿ αὑ[τ]οὺς πραγμάτων, οἵτινες ἐν τῇ φιλίᾳ τῇ ἡμετέρᾳ ἐνέμειναν, ὅπως αὐτοῖς δοθῶσιν [ο]ἷς τὰ καθ᾿ αὑτοὺς πράγματα ἐξηγήσωνται, περὶ τούτου τοῦ πράγματος οὕτως ἔδοξεν· ὅπως Κόιντος Μαίνιος στρατηγὸς τῶν ἐκ τῆς συνκλήτου [π]έντε ἀποτάξῃ οἳ ἂν αὐτῷ ἐκ τῶν δημοσίων πρα[γμ]άτων καὶ τῆς ἰδίας πίστεως φαίνωνται.

Concerning those matters about which the citizens of Thisbae made representations. Concerning their own affairs: the following decision was taken concerning the proposal that those who remained true to our friendship should be given the facilities to conduct their own affairs; that our praetor/governor Quintus Maenius should delegate five members of the senate who seemed to him appropriate in the light of their public actions and individual good faith.

== Sample 2 – Greek New Testament

The following excerpt, [[ the beginning of the Gospel of John | john-1 ]], is rendered in a reconstructed pronunciation representing a progressive popular variety of Koiné in the early Christian era.[[ ^37 ]] Modernizing features include the loss of vowel length distinction, monophthongization, transition to stress accent, and raising of η. Also seen here are the bilabial fricative pronunciation of diphthongs αυ and ευ, loss of initial aspiration, fricative values for β and γ, and partial post-nasal voicing of voiceless stops.

<p lang="grc-x-biblical">Ἐν ἀρχῇ ἦν ὁ λόγος, καὶ ὁ λόγος ἦν πρὸς τὸν θεόν, καὶ θεὸς ἦν ὁ λόγος. οὗτος ἦν ἐν ἀρχῇ πρὸς τὸν θεόν. πάντα δι᾽ αὐτοῦ ἐγένετο, καὶ χωρὶς αὐτοῦ ἐγένετο οὐδὲ ἕν ὃ γέγονεν. ἐν αὐτῷ ζωὴ ἦν, καὶ ἡ ζωὴ ἦν τὸ φῶς τῶν ἀνθρώπων. καὶ τὸ φῶς ἐν τῇ σκοτίᾳ φαίνει, καὶ ἡ σκοτία αὐτὸ οὐ κατέλαβεν.

[[ In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God. | john-11 ]] He was in the beginning with God. All things were made by Him; and without Him was not anything made that was made. In Him was life, and the life was the light of men. And the light shines in darkness; and the darkness comprehended it not (ESV translation uses alternate wording: "the darkness has not overcome it".)

